# Configuration for the runner. Copy to runner.env; keep it private. The variables were specified in
# https://github.com/Lementknight/zig-actions-runner/blob/7641a69/docs/SPEC.md §2, which predates
# RUNNER_TRUSTED_REFS, RUNNER_TRUSTED_EVENTS, RUNNER_ZIG_LOCAL_CACHE, RUNNER_ZIG_SEED,
# RUNNER_ZIG_SEED_VERSIONS and RUNNER_PREWARM; runner/src/config.zig (fromEnviron) reads them all.

# How the runner registers and removes its self-hosted runners: GH_TOKEN, or a GitHub App (below).
# One or the other: the runner refuses to start with both, or with neither. Empty counts as unset.
#
# A token that may manage self-hosted runners for GH_SCOPE: a fine-grained token with the
# repository permission "Administration: read and write" (an organization: "Self-hosted runners:
# read and write"), or a classic token with `repo` (`admin:org` for an organization).
GH_TOKEN=
# owner/repo, or an organization name.
GH_SCOPE=

# A GitHub App instead of GH_TOKEN: it reaches only the repositories it is installed on, and its
# installation token lasts an hour. The App needs, for a repository GH_SCOPE, the repository
# permission "Administration: read and write"; for an organization, the organization permission
# "Self-hosted runners: read and write". Install it on GH_SCOPE (its repository, or the org).
# The runner signs a JWT with the App's key (RS256, valid 10 minutes), exchanges it for an
# installation token (POST /app/installations/{id}/access_tokens), keeps that token in memory and
# mints the next one 5 minutes before it expires, or at once when GitHub answers 401. At startup it
# mints the first one and logs "GitHub App ...: installation N", or says why GitHub refused.
# Neither the key nor a token is ever logged.
#
# The App's ID (digits), or its Client ID (Iv23...): GitHub takes either as the JWT's issuer and
# now recommends the Client ID. Both are on the App's settings page; neither is secret.
# GH_APP_ID=
# The App's private key ("Generate a private key" on its settings page): the downloaded PEM, PKCS#1
# (BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY) as GitHub makes it, or PKCS#8 (BEGIN PRIVATE KEY). Give a path to it...
# GH_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/github-app.pem
# ...or the PEM itself, on one line with its line breaks written \n (env files hold one line per
# variable). Not both.
# GH_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIIE...\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----
# The installation's ID; unset, the runner looks it up at startup (GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/
# installation, or /orgs/{org}/installation).
# GH_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=
#
# Under Docker Compose (deploy/compose.yaml), mount the key file read-only into the runner
# service. The image is FROM scratch and the runner runs as root, so a key owned by root with mode
# 0400 on the host is readable inside and by nobody else on it:
#
#   sudo install -o root -g root -m 0400 app.pem /etc/zig-runner/github-app.pem
#
#   services:
#     runner:
#       volumes:
#         - /etc/zig-runner/github-app.pem:/run/secrets/github-app.pem:ro
#
# with GH_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/github-app.pem here.

# prod (README, "Environments"): run only jobs from these refs, whose workflow file comes from one
# of them too, and from these events; a trailing * matches any rest. Fail everything else before
# any step runs. Unset or empty trusts no ref, so every job fails, and a runner with the prod label
# refuses to start. `*` trusts every ref: only on a machine untrusted code may reach (pre-prod).
RUNNER_TRUSTED_REFS=refs/heads/main,refs/tags/v*
# Unset: push,workflow_dispatch. `*` trusts every event; empty, none.
# RUNNER_TRUSTED_EVENTS=push,workflow_dispatch
# Give steps the job's Dagger engine (privileged nesting, for `dagger call` inside a step), only in
# jobs the runner vouches for (the refs and events above). That access runs privileged containers
# and opens any cache volume by its key, which is control of this machine. Off by default; the
# runner logs "Dagger in steps: on" or "off" at startup.
# RUNNER_DAGGER_NESTING=true

# How many jobs this machine runs at once, at most.
RUNNER_COUNT=4
# On a stop (SIGTERM), seconds a running job gets to finish before it is cancelled. Keep the
# service's TimeoutStopSec (compose: stop_grace_period) about 5 minutes above it.
# RUNNER_STOP_GRACE_SECS=600

# The memory gate (#126): a runner asks GitHub for a job only while the machine has the memory for
# it; otherwise it removes its registration and waits, looking again every 5 s or as soon as a job
# starts or ends, and logs once when it holds back and once when it goes on. Running jobs are never
# touched. On by default; each limit below is turned off by 0. Four `zig build test` jobs taken at
# once on razorbill (8 GB, the engine capped at 4 GB) filled the engine and its swap, and the Pi
# thrashed for hours.
# RUNNER_ADMISSION=true
# MemAvailable (/proc/meminfo) that must be left after reserving RUNNER_MEM_PER_JOB_MB for this job,
# for each runner of the process already polling, and for each job started in the last 2 minutes
# (its memory does not show yet). razorbill idles at about 5.5 GB available, so three runners
# poll at once (1536 + 3 x 1024 = 4608) and a fourth waits until one job's memory shows: four
# heavy jobs (about 1 GB each, with the engine's 600 MB) do not fit under the engine's 4 GB cap,
# three do. Light jobs are not slowed beyond that: two minutes in, only their real use counts.
# Where the engine's cgroup is found (below), a job whose name has run before reserves, while it
# starts, the most its last 5 runs raised the engine's memory by, instead of RUNNER_MEM_PER_JOB_MB
# (#144): a uv job 300 MB, `zig build test` 1.6 GB. Runs that shared the engine with another job
# measured both, so they count only until the job has run alone (the least of them, until then).
# A runner polling, or a job not seen yet, still reserves RUNNER_MEM_PER_JOB_MB, or the most any
# job was seen to take if that is more. What was learned is in memory only: after a restart every
# job reserves the worst case until it has run.
# Runners in another process (a second pool) are not counted: one pool per machine is best.
# RUNNER_MIN_MEM_AVAILABLE_MB=1536
# RUNNER_MEM_PER_JOB_MB=1024
# RUNNER_MEM_PER_JOB_MB for native jobs (RUNNER_BACKEND=native, docs/native-backend.md P4): what a
# job not yet run natively reserves, and, on a native pool, what a runner polling reserves (or the
# most any native job took, if that is more). A native job is measured in its own cgroup, with
# nothing of the engine's in it, and learned apart from the same job on Dagger. Lower than the
# engine's because its cgroup holds a job that outgrows it: memory.max
# (RUNNER_NATIVE_MEMORY_MAX_MB) is the hard cap, and the floor above still applies. Where jobs can
# swap (RUNNER_NATIVE_SWAP), each one's memory.high is twice its estimate plus 512 MB (1 GB for a
# job not learned yet), past which the kernel reclaims and slows that job. With no swap, the
# default, that would stop a job dead, so it is not set, and a job not learned yet reserves the
# most any native job took while it ramps up, not this. 256 covers a
# shell, checkout, uv or npm job (a few MB to 300 MB; page cache is not counted), and six runners
# of it are razorbill's 1536 MB floor: its six native runners held e2e jobs of a few MB for up to
# 150 s behind 1.5 GB each before. 0 reserves nothing for native jobs and sets no memory.high.
# RUNNER_NATIVE_MEM_PER_JOB_MB=256
# Memory pressure, PSI `some avg10` (/proc/pressure/memory), in percent: how much of the last 10 s
# some task spent waiting for memory. It catches what MemAvailable misses, the engine swapping at
# its own cgroup cap while the host has memory left. A healthy machine sits at 0; thrashing is
# over 50. Resumes under half. No signal where PSI is off (`psi=0`, or a kernel without it).
# RUNNER_MAX_MEM_PRESSURE=10
# Swap growth, in MB a minute: for kernels without PSI, and the engine cgroup's own swap
# (memory.swap.current) below. Resumes under half. Swap merely in use is not counted: pages stay
# there long after the burst that put them there.
# RUNNER_MAX_SWAP_GROWTH_MB=64
# The engine's own memory cap (#142): the same reservations, against the engine container's cgroup.
# With 4 jobs in flight razorbill's engine hit its 4 GB cap and swapped 1 GB in 30 s while the host
# still showed 2 GB available (4.8 GB as each job was let through). In use is memory.current less
# inactive_file and slab_reclaimable: the engine's inactive file cache and the kernel's dentry and
# inode cache, which it drops first, hold nobody. `docker stats` subtracts only the first, so it
# reads 200-300 MB higher than the log on the Pi. This much must be left under the cap after the
# reservations. The engine idles at about 1 GB of 4 GB by this measure (its own heap about 650 MB
# plus the files it is reading; the 2026-10-31 sweep, to be re-checked against the next soak): two
# jobs (256 + 2 x 1024 = 2304) fit from 1 GB, three (3328) from under 770 MB; a fourth waits.
# Resumes with 256 MB more.
# RUNNER_MIN_ENGINE_HEADROOM_MB=256
# Unset: found through RUNNER_ENGINE_HOST's unix socket (the process listening on it, its
# /proc/<pid>/cgroup, then the nearest cgroup above with a memory.max), with no Docker access. The
# log says which ("the engine's cgroup is ..., capped at 4.0 GB"), or why not, and the gate then
# sees the host only: an engine over TCP or through Docker, one with no cap, or a runner in a
# container that cannot see the engine's processes (deploy/compose.yaml). Not found is retried
# every minute, so an engine that starts after the runner is picked up. A cgroup directory sets
# it; `none` turns it off.
# RUNNER_ENGINE_CGROUP=/sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/docker-<container id>.scope
# Seconds a step may wait with nothing at all heard from the engine (no health check, which it
# sends every 5 s, no answer, no log line) before its session is aborted and the step failed
# (#100). A swapping engine answers late, not that late; 0 turns it off.
# RUNNER_ENGINE_SILENCE_TIMEOUT=600
# Labels jobs select with `runs-on: [self-hosted, prod]`.
RUNNER_LABELS=self-hosted,linux,zig,prod
RUNNER_NAME_PREFIX=zig

# Steps run as `runner` (uid 1001, passwordless sudo), as on GitHub; `root` runs them as root.
# RUNNER_STEP_USER=runner
# Experimental: replay the steps after a secret-using step when its output is unchanged.
# RUNNER_OUTPUT_KEYS=false
# Their cost follows the size of the job's filesystem, not of what changed (1 s on a small
# checkout, 25 s on a large one). A job over this many milliseconds turns them off for that
# repository until the runner restarts; 0 never skips.
# RUNNER_OUTPUT_KEYS_MAX_MS=10000
# uv's project environment lives in uv's cache volume, so uv hardlinks instead of copying every
# file (28.6 s -> 0.5 s for flask on a Raspberry Pi); the workspace's .venv links to it.
# Only in jobs without step caching: with it, the environment stays in the workspace, so a
# replayed `uv sync` restores it exactly instead of leaving another commit's dependencies.
# RUNNER_UV_ENV_IN_CACHE=true
# Keep Zig's local cache (.zig-cache) between jobs, in a cache volume per repository. Only for the
# jobs the runner vouches for (the `repo` scope): an untrusted job's is its own.
# RUNNER_ZIG_LOCAL_CACHE=false
# Zig grows that cache by about 111 MB for every commit built and never removes anything, and part
# of a Zig cache cannot be removed safely. Past this many MB the next job starts it over, whole, in
# a new generation of the volume (a new commit recompiles anyway: about 6 s); a generation goes 6
# hours after it stops being current. 0 keeps one unbounded cache in the volume's root, as before.
# RUNNER_ZIG_LOCAL_CACHE_MAX_MB=4096
# Seed the Zig global cache of jobs that run Zig with Zig 0.17's build runner, built once per
# engine (104 s on one core; a cold `zig build test` of zig-runner went from 146 s to 49 s). On by
# default: the releases to seed, comma-separated, and the Zig versions found in `repo` jobs' tool
# caches, at most 3 in all, each tarball's minisign signature checked against Zig's pinned key
# before it is used; one that does not verify is not seeded. The default here is the Zig the
# runner is built with.
# RUNNER_ZIG_SEED_VERSIONS=0.17.0
# Or pin one release by digest instead (no signature check: the digest is your pin).
# <version>=<sha256 of zig-<arch>-linux-<version>.tar.xz for this machine>, from
# https://ziglang.org/download/index.json. On x86_64:
# RUNNER_ZIG_SEED=0.17.0=1cbe9df9f27e6b78d14ccbca43b6703a404ef79ef1c463de901d7f088d4e2026
# On aarch64 (a Raspberry Pi):
# RUNNER_ZIG_SEED=0.17.0=9e8d11661d4ae3bd57702a3832781e23ad151dde5798e16a5ccd503f65234ff8

# Pre-warm: build the default image's provisioned base container, and the Zig seed above, in the
# background when the runner starts, when the engine starts again (a restart, when its cache can be
# wiped) and after a job that had to wait for the engine, so the first job does not spend minutes
# in "Set up job" on them. It logs "pre-warm: base container ... built in N s (<image@digest>)". It
# goes through the memory gate as a job named "zig-runner pre-warm", mounts no volume and runs no
# step; a failed warm is logged and the first job builds what it needs. Skipped with
# RUNNER_ENGINE=cli and when the runner trusts no ref. On by default. Runners that share an engine
# warm one at a time: each takes a lock (flock) on the directory of the engine's unix socket, so
# the next logs "pre-warm waiting for another runner's warm on this engine" and then finds it all
# cached. Nothing to set up: reading that directory is enough, as under ProtectSystem=strict and
# with the compose engine-socket volume.
# RUNNER_PREWARM=false

# Optional: what bounds native jobs (docs/native-backend.md, "Bounded jobs"). None of this needs
# setting: all of a machine's native jobs together stay under its memory less a third (2 to 12
# GiB), each job under two thirds of that, and none of them swaps: 5.3 and 3.6 GiB of 8 GiB, 20 and
# 13.3 GiB of 30 GiB. A job that needs more fails, saying which limit it reached. Every runner
# sharing a parent cgroup must use the same values: one set otherwise changes nothing while jobs
# run there, and says so. 0 is no limit, for all jobs too if it is the job's.
# RUNNER_NATIVE_MEMORY_TOTAL_MB=20480
# RUNNER_NATIVE_MEMORY_MAX_MB=13653
# RUNNER_NATIVE_SWAP=false
# Each job's processes and threads, and four times that for all jobs. 0 is no limit.
# RUNNER_NATIVE_PIDS_MAX=8192
# Where the jobs' cgroups go: as root, /sys/fs/cgroup/zig-runner, or `self` in a container at the
# root of its cgroup namespace. `self` is the runner's own, which it needs delegated, as under
# `systemd-run --user --scope -p Delegate=yes`. A path keeps them in its `jobs` child, and its own
# limits. A job that cannot have a cgroup does not run natively. `none` accepts that: jobs then run
# with no memory or process limit, and can take the machine down.
# RUNNER_NATIVE_CGROUP=self

# Optional: no Dagger engine at all (docs/native-backend.md, "Running with no engine"). Needs
# RUNNER_BACKEND=native. The pre-warm and the gate's look for the engine's cgroup are skipped, and a
# job native cannot run fails at once, saying why, instead of waiting for an engine. See also
# deploy/compose.engineless.yaml. Default: direct.
# RUNNER_ENGINE=none

# Optional: run state and logs in Postgres / Tiger Data, for the read API and web UI.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/tsdb?sslmode=require
